Executive Summary
This report presents a complete design and implementation strategy for a responsive, accessible, and privacy-compliant web page featuring a Black-Hole animation, an anime-style planetary theme for language selection, a subscription form, and a final page with an Apple-like UI. Key deliverables include full HTML/CSS/JS source code, asset references, AI image prompts for the graphics, deployment instructions, optimization notes, and a testing checklist. Emphasis is placed on vanilla HTML/CSS/JS (no heavy frameworks), minimal dependencies, cross-browser support, and graceful degradation (e.g. prefers-reduced-motion media query for animation). We compare implementation options (static+form service vs. Node.js), list royalty-free image sources, and include mermaid diagrams illustrating page flow and components. Throughout, we cite official references (MDN, W3C, etc.) for web standards and best practices.
1. Black Hole Animation
An animated black hole effect can be achieved with CSS and HTML (or Canvas/JS for more complex visuals). A simple yet effective approach uses a rotating gradient “accretion disk” behind a central dark circle. For example:
code
Html
<div id="blackhole-container">
  <div class="acc-disk"></div>
  <div class="hole"></div>
</div>
code
CSS
#blackhole-container {
  position: relative;
  width: 300px; height: 300px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}
.acc-disk {
  width: 100%; height: 100%;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(200,200,200,1) 10%, rgba(0,0,0,0.8) 70%);
  animation: spin 8s linear infinite;
}
.hole {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%; left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  width: 60%; height: 60%;
  background: #000;
  border-radius: 50%;
  box-shadow: 0 0 40px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.7);
}
@keyframes spin {
  from { transform: rotate(0deg); }
  to   { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}
This creates a dark central “hole” with a rotating ring. For more realism, add multiple <div> rings with CSS animations or use <canvas> for particle effects. In all cases, respect the prefers-reduced-motion setting: use a media query to disable or simplify animation for users who have requested reduced motion.
Graceful degradation: If CSS animations aren’t supported or motion is reduced, the disk can remain static or fallback to a static image. Using @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .acc-disk { animation: none; } } ensures compliance.
Cross-browser: The above CSS relies on standard features. The transform and animation properties are supported in all modern browsers (see MDN compatibility). Always test on major browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge).
Performance: Use will-change: transform on animated elements to enable GPU acceleration. Limit DOM elements and avoid heavy canvas loops on mobile. Serve optimized assets (minified CSS/JS, compressed images).
Fallback: Provide a static background image or simplified effect if JavaScript/CSS fails. For example, wrap the animation in a <noscript> tag with a static image.
Related Resources: MDN’s guide to CSS animations and media queries provides syntax examples. Apple’s sites often use subtle animations; however, any motion should be optional (as per WCAG and prefers-reduced-motion).
